<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerei Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Kerey Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kerei Khan was a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urus-khan">Urus Khan</a> of the White Horde and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janibek-khan">Janibek Khan</a>, the founder of the Kazakh Khanate. In the mid-15th century the two led discontented clans away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> confederation to the Chu valley, under the protection of the khans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>. Kerei is traditionally counted the first khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerei-khan.jpg" alt="Kerei Khan" width="312" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Bank of Kazakhstan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KZ-2015-500tenge-Khanate-b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kerei was born in 1424. In the late 1450s part of the nomadic population, led by Janibek and Kerei, broke away from the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> khan Abu'l-Khayr and migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, settling in the valleys of the Chu and Talas rivers, where the khan of Moghulistan offered them support against their opponents. Around 200,000 nomads joined the movement, and its strength alarmed Abu'l-Khayr. He set out on a campaign against Moghulistan in 1468 but died suddenly on the way.</p><p>His son Sheikh Haidar succeeded him, but all of Abu'l-Khayr's opponents combined against the new khan, whose short reign ended when the Siberian Khan Ibak murdered him. Kerei then took supreme power in the eastern Dasht and appointed his brother Janibek ruler of the west wing. The two khans still had to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> for power over the eastern steppe, and Kerei and his followers fought for an independent state. Kerei was killed in that conflict; the sources mention him for the last time in the events of the winter of 1473.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The secession led by Kerei and Janibek is seen as an important link in the chain of events that produced the Kazakh Khanate and the Kazakh nationality itself. From the end of the 15th century the name Kazakh took on a political sense, designating the domains created by the two khans, and from the beginning of the 16th century, after part of the tribes moved with Shaibani Khan into Maverannahr, an ethnic one.</p><p>Why Kerei and Janibek broke away, and where the khanate first took shape, are unknown, and a number of authors date its formation to the turn of the 15th and 16th centuries, treating the migration as only its beginning. On 1 June 2010 a monument to Kerei and Janibek by the sculptor Renat Abenov was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a>, near the Museum of the First President of Kazakhstan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kerei Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two sultans of the rival line of Urus Khan, <b>Kerei</b> and his kinsman Janibek, broke with him, and around 1465, the date given by the historian Muhammad Haidar Dughlat, they led their followers south-east into Moghulistan, where Esen Buqa Khan allowed them to settle in the western Chu…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1457 his army was badly defeated by the Oirats, and discontented clans under <b>Kerei</b> and Janibek broke away to form the Kazakh Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the White Horde. Died in the Kazakh Khanate. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
